== The Preface

THAT the Doctrine of #emph[Original Sin,] is one of the fundamental Truths of our Christian Profession, hath been always owned in the Church of God. And an especial part it is of that peculiar possession of truth, which they enjoy, whose Religion towards God is built upon, and resolved into Divine Revelation. As the world by its #emph[Wisdom] never knew God aright, so the Wise Men of it were always utterly ignorant of this inbred evil in themselves and others. With us the #emph[Doctrine] and Conviction of it, lie in the very foundation of all wherein we have to do with God, in reference unto our pleasing of him here, or obtaining the enjoyment of him hereafter. It is also known what influence it hath into the great truths concerning the #emph[Person] of Christ, his mediation, the fruits and effects of it, with all the benefits that we are made partakers of thereby. Without a supposition of it, not any of them can be truly known, or savingly believed. For this cause hath it been largely treated of by many holy and #emph[Learned Men,] both of old and of latter days. Some have labored in the discovery of #emph[its nature,] some of its #emph[guilt] and demerit; by whom also the #emph[truth] concerning it hath been vindicated from the opposition made unto it, in the past and present Ages. By most these things have been considered in their #emph[full extent] and latitude, with respect unto all men by nature, with the estate and condition of them who are wholly under the power and guilt of it. How thereby men are disenabled and incapacitated in themselves to answer the obedience required either in the Law, or the Gospel, so as to free themselves from the #emph[Curse] of the one or to make themselves partakers of the #emph[blessing] of the other, hath been by many also fully evinced. Moreover, that there are #emph[Remainders] of it abiding in #emph[Believers] after their Regeneration and Conversion to God, as the Scripture abundantly testifies; so it hath been fully taught and confirmed; as also how the #emph[guilt] of it is pardoned unto them, and by what means the #emph[power] of it is weakened in them. All these things I say have been largely treated on, to the great benefit and edification of the Church. In what we have now in design, we therefore take them all for #emph[granted,] and endeavor only farther to carry on the discovery of it in its actings and oppositions to the #emph[Law] and #emph[Grace] of God in Believers. Neither do I intend the discussing of any thing that hath been controverted about it. What the #emph[Scripture] plainly revealeth & teacheth concerning it, what Believers evidently find by #emph[experience] in themselves, what they may learn from the Examples and acknowledgments of others, shall be represented in a way suited unto the capacity of the meanest and weakest who is concerned therein. And many things seem to render the handling of it at this season, not unnecessary. The Effects and fruits of it which we see in the Apostasies and backslidings of many, the scandalous sins and miscarriages of some, and the course and lives of the most, seem to call for a due consideration of it. Besides of how great concernment a full and clear acquaintance with the power of this #emph[Indwelling-Sin] (the matter designed to be opened) is unto Believers, to stir them up to watchfulness and diligence, to faith and prayer, to call them to repentance, humility, & self-abasement, will appear in our progress. These in general were the ends aimed at in the ensuing Discourse, which being at first composed and delivered for the use and benefit of a #emph[few,] is now by the Providence of God made public. And if the #emph[Reader] receive any advantage by these weak endeavors, let him know that it is his duty, as to give Glory unto God, so to help them by his prayers, who in many temptations and Afflictions are willing to labor in the Vineyard of the Lord, unto which work they are called.
